# Grade 11 (part a): teaching scripts, student steps and saint stories.
R = {
'11-1-1': dict(
  saint="Hippolytus of Rome: a priest and writer who recorded how the Church prayed in the early 200s.",
  tryit=["Read the opening dialogue of the Eucharistic Prayer.", "Mark who speaks.", "Say whose action the liturgy is."],
  saintStory="Hippolytus was a priest and teacher in Rome in the early third century, a learned man who wrote on many subjects. He disagreed with several popes, was elected a rival bishop of Rome by his followers, and spent years outside the main body of the Church. During the persecution under Emperor Maximinus Thrax, around 235, he and Pope Pontian were both exiled to the mines of Sardinia. There they were reconciled, and both died of the hardships.\nHis best-known work, the Apostolic Tradition, describes how the Roman Church worshiped: the ordination of bishops, priests and deacons, the preparation and Baptism of converts, and a Eucharistic Prayer. It begins with a dialogue still used today: “The Lord be with you. And with your spirit. Lift up your hearts. We lift them up to the Lord. Let us give thanks to the Lord. It is right and just.”\nThe Church honors him as a martyr and a witness to the liturgy. His text shows how much of the Mass we pray today goes back to the early Church.",
  script=dict(
    open="Read aloud the opening dialogue of the Eucharistic Prayer from the Missal, back and forth with the class. Ask, “How old do you think these words are? Who is speaking, and to whom?”",
    teach=["Say: “The word ‘liturgy’ comes from the Greek leitourgia, ‘public work’ or ‘service of the people.’ In the Church it means the participation of the People of God in the work of God. Vatican II’s Sacrosanctum Concilium says that in the liturgy, ‘the work of our redemption is accomplished.’ (SC 2) Christ is always present in his Church, especially in her liturgical actions: in the person of the minister, in the Eucharistic species, in his word, and when the community prays and sings. (SC 7) So the liturgy is not a performance by the priest, or merely a human gathering. It is the work of Christ the High Priest, with his Body, the Church.”", "Say: “The liturgy is the action of the whole Trinity. The Father is the source and goal: we bless him as Creator. The Son makes present the Paschal Mystery, his death and Resurrection; he is the priest and the sacrifice. The Holy Spirit prepares the Church to meet Christ, recalls the mystery to mind (anamnesis), makes it present, and unites us in communion. Look at the structure of the Eucharistic Prayer: we thank the Father (preface), we remember Christ’s saving work (anamnesis), we call down the Spirit (epiclesis) and we offer, intercede and glorify in the doxology: ‘Through him, and with him, and in him.’”", "Say: “The liturgy also anticipates heaven. Revelation 4-5 pictures the heavenly liturgy: the Lamb, the elders, the angels, the song ‘Holy, holy, holy.’ In the Mass we join this. The Church year unfolds the mystery of Christ: Advent, Christmas, Lent, the Triduum, Easter, Ordinary Time. The Easter Vigil is the ‘mother of all vigils.’ Vatican II also taught that the liturgy is ‘the summit toward which the activity of the Church is directed; it is also the font from which all her power flows.’ (SC 10) Hence the call for ‘full, conscious and active participation’ (SC 14): not just doing things but offering ourselves with Christ. Hippolytus’s text shows this is ancient.”"],
    ask=["What does ‘liturgy’ mean? (Public work or service of the people.)", "Who is the principal agent of the liturgy? (Christ, the High Priest, with his Body the Church.)", "What are the roles of the Trinity in the liturgy? (Father is source, Son makes present the Paschal Mystery, Spirit prepares and unites.)"],
    practice=["Read the Eucharistic Prayer’s preface dialogue and mark who speaks each line.", "Make a chart: Father, Son, Holy Spirit. Under each, find where they are named or acted in the Eucharistic Prayer.", "Identify four moments where the Mass echoes Revelation 4-5.", "Write a sentence: “At Mass, the real actor is…”"],
    close="Pray the preface dialogue together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pay attention to the words ‘Lift up your hearts’ at Mass.”")),

'11-1-2': dict(
  saint="Martin of Tours: a soldier who cut his cloak for a beggar and saw Christ wearing it.",
  tryit=["Analyze one sacrament as matter, form, minister and effect.", "Test whether each part is necessary.", "Say what the sign shows."],
  saintStory="Martin was born around 316 in what is now Hungary, the son of a Roman officer. He was enrolled in the army at fifteen, and posted in Gaul. He was a catechumen, someone preparing for Baptism, though he had not yet been baptized. One winter day, at the gates of Amiens, he met a beggar who was nearly naked and shivering. Martin had nothing but his weapons and his cloak. He drew his sword, cut the cloak in two and gave half to the man.\nThat night he dreamed of Christ wearing the half-cloak, saying to the angels, “Martin, who is still a catechumen, has clothed me.” Martin was baptized soon after, and left the army, saying, “I am a soldier of Christ; I am not allowed to fight.” He became a disciple of Hilary of Poitiers, a monk, and in 371 the bishop of Tours, where he lived simply in a cell, and traveled to bring the Gospel to rural people.\nHe died in 397. The half-cloak, or cappa, was kept as a relic, giving the word “chapel.” His act shows how a visible sign can carry an invisible reality.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hold up a plain cup of water and say, “This is water. Now suppose a priest pours it on a child’s head and says certain words. What do Catholics say happens? What is the difference between the water in my cup and the water at the font?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “A sacrament is ‘an efficacious sign of grace, instituted by Christ and entrusted to the Church, by which divine life is dispensed to us.’ (CCC 1131) ‘Sign’ because it is something visible that points to something invisible. ‘Efficacious’ because it actually accomplishes what it signifies. ‘Instituted by Christ’ because only he can attach grace to a sign. ‘Entrusted to the Church’ because she celebrates and dispenses them. The Church has always recognized seven: Baptism, Confirmation, Eucharist, Penance, Anointing, Holy Orders, Matrimony. The Council of Trent defined that there are seven, and not more or fewer, and that all were instituted by Christ.”", "Say: “Each sacrament has matter and form. The matter is the material element or action (water, bread and wine, oil, laying on of hands, the exchange of consent). The form is the words that give the matter its meaning (‘I baptize you in the name of the Father…’). Together they make the sign. The minister: usually a bishop, priest or deacon, but the spouses are ministers of Matrimony, and anyone may baptize in an emergency. The minister must have the intention of doing what the Church does. If any essential part is missing, the sacrament is invalid. For instance, baptizing with juice instead of water, or changing the Trinitarian formula, does not baptize.”", "Say: “How do sacraments work? The Church speaks of ex opere operato, ‘by the work done.’ The sacrament confers grace by the very fact of the action being performed, because Christ is acting, and not because of the personal holiness of the minister. A priest in a state of sin still validly consecrates the Eucharist and forgives sins. This protects us: we don’t depend on the minister’s worthiness. But the fruit received depends on our disposition. Three sacraments (Baptism, Confirmation, Holy Orders) confer a character, an indelible spiritual mark; they can’t be repeated. The sacraments are ‘sacraments of faith’: they presuppose, nourish and express faith. Aquinas called them ‘signs that cause what they signify.’”"],
    ask=["What is a sacrament? (An efficacious sign of grace, instituted by Christ and entrusted to the Church.)", "What are matter and form? (The material element and the words.)", "What does ex opere operato mean? (The sacrament works by Christ’s action, not the minister’s holiness.)"],
    practice=["Make a table of the seven sacraments with columns: matter, form, minister, effect.", "Fill in as much as you can, then check with the Catechism.", "Mark the three that confer a character.", "Choose one and write what would make it invalid."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, you meet us in visible signs. Give us faith to see.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Look for the sign in each sacrament you see this month.”")),

'11-1-3': dict(
  saint="Stanislaus Kostka: a Polish teenager who walked to Rome to become a Jesuit and received Communion with extraordinary devotion.",
  tryit=["List obstacles to the fruit of a sacrament.", "List what helps it.", "Write your preparation plan."],
  saintStory="Stanislaus Kostka was born in 1550 into a noble Polish family. At fourteen, he and his older brother Paul were sent to study in Vienna, at a Jesuit school. Stanislaus was serious about his faith, which his brother mocked. When he fell dangerously ill, the Lutheran family where he lodged would not allow a priest to bring him the Eucharist. Stanislaus is said to have prayed to St. Barbara, patron of the dying, and an angel brought him Communion. Recovered, he was determined to become a Jesuit.\nThe Austrian Jesuits could not accept him without his father’s permission, which they knew he would not give. So Stanislaus left Vienna in disguise, and walked about 350 miles to Augsburg and then to Rome, where the superior general, Francis Borgia, accepted him in 1567.\nHe lived as a novice for only ten months. He died in August 1568, at seventeen, after a short illness. He is the patron of students and novices, and shows what a heart disposed for the sacraments is like.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Two people attend the same Mass. One leaves unchanged; the other is moved. What might account for the difference?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Sacraments work ex opere operato, but they do not work like magic. The fruit received depends on the disposition of the recipient. The Catechism: ‘The fruits of the sacraments also depend on the disposition of the one who receives them.’ (CCC 1128) Disposition means the state of heart and mind: faith, repentance, openness, the intention to receive what the Church gives. A sacrament received validly but without proper disposition may be valid, but its fruits are blocked, until the obstacle is removed. For example, a person who is baptized as an adult without any intention of repenting; the character is imprinted, but the grace awaits.”", "Say: “Obstacles. Mortal sin: a person who receives Communion in mortal sin commits sacrilege (1 Corinthians 11:27-29), and must first be reconciled in Confession. Lack of faith or intention. Hypocrisy, holding a grudge, or hidden hostility. Distraction and routine, which won’t invalidate but diminish. What helps: prayer before and after; examination of conscience; fasting; recollection; asking the Spirit’s help; making the effort to come early; confessing sins; reconciling with those we’ve wronged; and thanksgiving. We can compare it to sunlight on a window: the sun shines, but a dirty window keeps out the light.”", "Say: “Sacraments are also fruitful in time. The grace given in Baptism or Confirmation can ‘revive’ when a person later repents (reviviscence). The seeds of grace need cultivation. Sacraments also have effects that are ecclesial: they build the Church. Receiving the Eucharist unworthily harms not just oneself but the community. Remember the parable of the sower. The word is the same, the seed is the same; the soil determines the yield. Pope Pius X’s encouragement of frequent Communion came with the condition of right intention and state of grace. And Stanislaus prepared with great fervor. We should treat the sacraments not as routine but as encounters with Christ.”"],
    ask=["Do sacraments work like magic? (No; fruit depends on our disposition.)", "What is an obstacle to the fruit? (Mortal sin, lack of faith, hypocrisy, distraction.)", "What helps? (Prayer, examination, Confession, reconciliation, thanksgiving.)"],
    practice=["Make two lists: obstacles and helps for receiving Communion fruitfully.", "Write a 5-step routine for preparing for Mass (night before, morning, arriving, during, after).", "Mark which step you most often skip.", "Commit to one change this month."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, make my heart a good soil.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Prepare for Mass for five minutes before leaving home.”")),

'11-2-1': dict(
  saint="Remigius of Reims: the bishop who baptized Clovis and the Franks.",
  tryit=["Trace the effects of Baptism in the Catechism.", "Match each to the rite.", "Answer a skeptic on infant Baptism."],
  saintStory="Remigius was born around 437 in what is now France, and became bishop of Reims at about twenty-two. He lived through the collapse of Roman rule in Gaul and the arrival of the Franks, a pagan Germanic people. In 481, Clovis became king of the Franks at fifteen. Clovis married Clotilde, a Catholic princess, who prayed for his conversion. During a battle against the Alemanni in 496, Clovis, seeing defeat, vowed that if the God of Clotilde gave him victory, he would be baptized.\nHe won, and on Christmas Day, about 496, Remigius baptized Clovis and, it is said, three thousand of his warriors. According to tradition, Remigius told him, “Bow your head, proud Sicambrian. Burn what you have worshiped, worship what you have burned.”\nThe baptism of Clovis led to the conversion of the Franks and a Catholic France. Remigius died around 533. Whether or not Clovis was fully converted in heart, the story shows how Baptism is a decisive entry into the Church.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If Baptism only washed away original sin, why would the early Church baptize at Easter with a whole night of preparation? What else is happening?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Baptism is the first of the sacraments, the ‘door’ to life in the Spirit. It is necessary for salvation (John 3:5, ‘unless one is born of water and the Spirit, he cannot enter the kingdom of God’). The Catechism lists its effects. One: forgiveness of all sin, original sin and personal sins, and all punishment for sin. Two: new birth as children of God, sharing in the divine nature, members of Christ and temples of the Spirit. Three: incorporation into the Church, the Body of Christ. Four: an indelible spiritual character, a permanent sign of belonging to Christ. Five: the theological virtues and gifts of the Spirit. After Baptism a person still has concupiscence (inclination to sin), a struggle that continues.”", "Say: “The rite. The sign is water, either by immersion or pouring, with the Trinitarian formula. The rite includes: reception of the child, the Sign of the Cross, liturgy of the Word, the exorcism and anointing with the oil of catechumens, the blessing of the water, the renunciation of sin and profession of faith, the baptism itself, the anointing with chrism, the white garment, the lighted candle from the Easter candle, the Our Father, and the blessing. For adults, the rite of Christian initiation includes the catechumenate, the scrutinies in Lent, and baptism at the Easter Vigil, together with Confirmation and Eucharist. Each sign has biblical roots: water (creation, flood, Red Sea, Jordan), oil (anointing), white garment (Galatians 3:27, ‘put on Christ’).”", "Say: “Questions. Infant Baptism: the Church baptizes infants because it is a gift of grace, not a reward; parents and godparents promise to raise the child in the faith. The early Church practiced it (Origen says it comes from the apostles). Where an infant dies unbaptized, the Church entrusts to God’s mercy. Emergency Baptism: anyone may baptize with water and the right words and intention. Baptism of desire and blood. Baptism in other Christian churches is recognized. Who may be a godparent: a confirmed Catholic in good standing, at least sixteen. Why one name: Baptism gives each a Christian name. The Church ministers: bishop, priest, deacon. Baptism isn’t just a ceremony but a death and rising with Christ.”"],
    ask=["Name three effects of Baptism. (Forgiveness of sin, new birth as God’s child, incorporation into the Church.)", "What is the character? (An indelible spiritual mark.)", "Who may baptize in an emergency? (Anyone with water, the Trinitarian formula and the right intention.)"],
    practice=["Match each rite element to its meaning and Bible text in a table.", "Write a short answer to a Protestant friend who says infant Baptism isn’t biblical.", "Find your own baptismal date, parish and godparents.", "Write a thank-you note to your godparent."],
    close="Renew your baptismal promises. Sign of the Cross with holy water. Send home: “Thank your godparents.”")),

'11-2-2': dict(
  saint="Chiara Luce Badano: a teenager who offered her illness for others and called it a gift.",
  tryit=["Read the rite of Confirmation.", "Match each sign with its meaning.", "Write your own preparation plan."],
  saintStory="Chiara Badano was born in 1971 in northern Italy, after her parents had prayed for a child for eleven years. She loved sports, music and friends. At age nine she joined the Focolare Movement, which emphasizes unity and love. When she was seventeen, while playing tennis, she felt a sharp pain in her shoulder. She was diagnosed with bone cancer.\nShe was frightened, and for the first hours she said nothing. Then she said, “If you want it, Jesus, I want it too.” She accepted the treatment, refused morphine because it would dull her mind, saying she wanted to offer her pain to Jesus. Visitors came to cheer her up and left comforted themselves. She planned her funeral, choosing her dress, which she called her wedding gown, and the songs. She gave her corneas to a donor.\nShe died on October 7, 1990, at eighteen. Her last words to her mother: “Goodbye. Be happy, because I am.” She was beatified in 2010. She shows the courage that Confirmation gives.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Confirmation is often called ‘the sacrament of Christian maturity.’ Is that accurate? What is it, if not graduation from religious ed?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Confirmation completes Baptism. The Catechism says: ‘By the sacrament of Confirmation, [the baptized] are more perfectly bound to the Church and are enriched with a special strength of the Holy Spirit. Hence they are, as true witnesses of Christ, more strictly obliged to spread and defend the faith by word and deed.’ (CCC 1285) The Scriptural basis: Pentecost (Acts 2), the laying on of hands at Samaria (Acts 8:14-17), at Ephesus (Acts 19:6). It is the sacrament of the gift of the Spirit. It deepens baptismal grace; roots us more deeply as God’s children; unites us more firmly to Christ; increases the gifts of the Spirit in us; makes our bond with the Church more perfect; and gives special strength to witness.”", "Say: “The rite. The essential rite is the anointing with sacred chrism, accompanied by the laying on of the minister’s hand and the words, ‘Be sealed with the Gift of the Holy Spirit.’ The chrism is olive oil mixed with balsam perfume, consecrated by the bishop at the Chrism Mass during Holy Week. The oil signifies strength, consecration and the fragrance of Christ. The Latin Church ordinarily gives Confirmation to those who have reached the age of discretion; the age varies, often ages 12-16 in the United States. The Eastern Churches confirm infants right after Baptism. The ordinary minister is the bishop. The candidate chooses a sponsor, a confirmed Catholic, and often a Confirmation name. The candidate renews baptismal promises.”", "Say: “Confirmation’s place in the order of the sacraments of initiation (Baptism, Confirmation, Eucharist) is debated. In the early Church, they were given together. In the Western Church, they were separated. Today Confirmation is sometimes given before First Communion in some dioceses, to restore the original order. For adults entering the Church, they are given together at the Easter Vigil. What does Confirmation ask of us? To be witnesses: of Christ in school, work and family. To use our gifts for the common good. To live as people of the Spirit: love, joy, peace and so on. Like Chiara, we receive the courage to say yes to God’s will.”"],
    ask=["What does Confirmation do? (Completes Baptism; gives strength to witness.)", "What are the essential rite and words? (Anointing with chrism with the laying on of hands: ‘Be sealed with the Gift of the Holy Spirit.’)", "Who is the ordinary minister? (The bishop.)"],
    practice=["Draw a flowchart of the Confirmation rite with the meaning of each part.", "Write about the saint you would choose as your Confirmation patron and why.", "List three ways you will use the Spirit’s gifts to witness at school.", "Write a prayer asking for the Holy Spirit."],
    close="Pray the prayer to the Holy Spirit. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray daily for the Spirit’s gifts.”")),

'11-2-3': dict(
  saint="Paschal Baylon: a Spanish shepherd and Franciscan brother devoted to the Blessed Sacrament.",
  tryit=["Outline the three dimensions of the Eucharist.", "Pair each with a Scripture text.", "Write your own thanksgiving after Communion."],
  saintStory="Paschal Baylon was born in 1540 in Aragon, Spain, to poor farming parents. He worked as a shepherd from age seven. While tending sheep, he would pray and kneel facing the nearest church, when he heard the bell for Mass. He taught himself to read, so he could say prayers from a book, by asking passersby to teach him letters.\nAt twenty-four he joined the Franciscan Alcantarines as a lay brother. He was assigned to work as porter, cook and gardener, and served as a messenger, traveling on foot across France, which was then a dangerous journey for a Spanish friar, since the Huguenots persecuted Catholics. Once he was stoned for defending the Real Presence in a debate. He spent long hours before the Blessed Sacrament.\nHe died in 1592 on Pentecost Sunday, as Mass was being celebrated, at the moment of the elevation. In 1897, Pope Leo XIII named him patron of Eucharistic Congresses and societies.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If someone said, ‘The Eucharist is just a symbol,’ how would you respond, using only the Bible?” Let students try. “The earliest Christians had to answer this too.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Eucharist is ‘the source and summit of the Christian life.’ The Catechism describes the Eucharist under several names: Lord’s Supper, breaking of bread, Eucharistic assembly, memorial, holy sacrifice, Holy Communion, Holy Mass. It was instituted at the Last Supper (Matthew 26:26-28; 1 Corinthians 11:23-26). It has three aspects. Sacrifice and memorial: the Eucharist re-presents Christ’s sacrifice on the cross. ‘Do this in remembrance of me’ is anamnesis, which makes present. Hebrews 9-10 teaches that Christ’s sacrifice is once for all, and the Mass is not a new one. Real Presence: John 6:51-58 and the words of institution. Communion: union with Christ and each other.”", "Say: “The Real Presence. The Church teaches that the whole Christ is present: body, blood, soul and divinity, in each species, under the appearances of bread and wine. The term ‘transubstantiation’ was used to explain how: the substance (what a thing is) changes, while the accidents (appearances) remain. The Fourth Lateran Council (1215) used it; Trent confirmed. The Church’s evidence: John 6 (Jesus didn’t retract when many left); 1 Corinthians 10:16 and 11:27-29 (guilty of the body and blood); the unanimous witness of the Fathers (Ignatius, Justin, Irenaeus, Cyril of Jerusalem). Eucharistic miracles, like Lanciano, are signs but not proof.”", "Say: “The Eucharist builds the Church, and she is its fruit. The Church teaches that Communion is for those in communion with the Church and in the state of grace. Reverent reception is in the hand or on the tongue, standing or kneeling, with a bow before receiving. The fast is one hour. In the Eucharist we are united to Christ, receive grace to avoid sin, are forgiven venial sins, deepen our unity with the Church and are committed to the poor. Matthew 25 and Mass go together: ‘You cannot claim to honor the Body of Christ in the Eucharist while neglecting his body in the poor,’ as Chrysostom said. Adoration extends the Mass. Every Mass ends by sending us out.”"],
    ask=["Name three aspects of the Eucharist. (Sacrifice and memorial, real presence, communion.)", "What is transubstantiation? (The substance changes; appearances remain.)", "How does the Eucharist relate to the poor? (We cannot honor Christ’s body at the altar and ignore it in the poor.)"],
    practice=["Make a three-column chart: Sacrifice, Presence, Communion, with a Scripture text and a Church teaching for each.", "Write the strongest Biblical argument for the Real Presence.", "Write a thanksgiving prayer for after Communion (4-5 sentences).", "Share one phrase with a partner."],
    close="Pray the Adoro Te Devote (one verse) or “Jesus, I adore you.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Spend five minutes in silent thanksgiving after Communion.”")),

'11-3-1': dict(
  saint="Moses the Black: a former robber who became a desert monk and a teacher of mercy.",
  tryit=["Distinguish kinds of sin.", "Use the three conditions for mortal sin.", "Write what repentance requires."],
  saintStory="Moses was an Ethiopian slave in the fourth century who was dismissed by his master for stealing and violence. He became the leader of a gang of robbers in Egypt, a huge, fearsome man. According to the desert fathers, one day he was fleeing the authorities, and hid in the monastery in Scete. The monks’ way of life moved him. He stayed, repented and became a monk.\nHe struggled for years with temptations and with his past. He went to Abba Isidore, who took him out to the roof at dawn and showed him the sun rising: “Slowly, slowly, the light comes.” Over time, he became a wise elder. When a brother was judged for a fault, Moses came to the council carrying a leaking basket of sand. When asked, he said, “My sins are running out behind me and I do not see them, and today I am coming to judge the sins of another.” The brothers forgave the offender.\nMoses was killed by raiders around 405. He is the patron of nonviolence in the Church.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “What is the difference between making a mistake, doing wrong, and sinning? Are they the same?” Take answers. “The Church has a careful vocabulary.”",
    teach=["Say: “Sin is ‘an offense against reason, truth and right conscience; it is a failure in genuine love for God and neighbor’ (CCC 1849). The tradition has always seen that sin is first an offense against God (Psalm 51:4: ‘Against you, you only, have I sinned’), and also wounds our nature and the community. Origin: original sin and personal sin. Distinctions: mortal sin destroys charity and requires conversion, usually through Confession; venial sin weakens charity but doesn’t destroy it. Three conditions for mortal sin: grave matter, full knowledge, deliberate consent. Grave matter is specified by the Ten Commandments. Culpability can be lessened by ignorance, fear, habit or force.”", "Say: “Sins can be classified by their object (what is done), by the virtues they oppose, by commandments, by whether they are of thought, word, deed or omission. The seven capital sins are root vices. Sin has consequences: loss of charity, deprivation of grace, eternal punishment if unrepented, temporal punishment, and the formation of vices. Social sin: structures of sin that arise from personal sins. Sin spreads: we can cooperate in the sins of others by commanding, advising, praising or defending them. Pope John Paul II said the loss of the sense of sin is a mark of our age. Sin is real; the alternative would make forgiveness meaningless.”", "Say: “The answer to sin is mercy. Luke 15 gathers three parables: lost sheep, lost coin, lost son. God seeks the sinner. ‘There will be more joy in heaven over one sinner who repents.’ Mercy doesn’t deny sin; it forgives it. The prodigal returns and the father runs. The Catechism says: ‘There is no offense, however serious, that the Church cannot forgive.’ (CCC 982) Conversion is more than feeling bad: it is metanoia, a change of mind and heart. Pope Francis in Misericordiae Vultus calls God’s name mercy. Moses the Black, Augustine, Mary Magdalene and Peter show that mercy makes saints out of sinners.”"],
    ask=["What is sin? (An offense against reason, truth and right conscience; a failure of love.)", "What three conditions make a sin mortal? (Grave matter, full knowledge, deliberate consent.)", "Is any sin unforgivable by the Church? (No; the Church can forgive any sin.)"],
    practice=["Prepare scenarios with varying details (grave matter but unknowing; deliberate but small matter; compelled by fear).", "Decide for each: mortal, venial or not culpable? Which condition is missing?", "Write what repentance requires in each case.", "Pray Psalm 51 together."],
    close="Pray the Act of Contrition. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read the parable of the prodigal son.”")),
}
